# Tracing `new Chalk({level: 3}).red("foo")` vs `new Chalk({level: 0}).red("foo")`

## 0. Setup: how `level` gets onto the instance

`new Chalk(options)` (source/index.js:34-39) doesn't build a normal instance — its constructor
does `return chalkFactory(options)` (line 37), and because JS constructors that explicitly
return an object use that object instead of `this`, the "instance" is actually whatever
`chalkFactory` produces.

`chalkFactory` (source/index.js:41-48) creates the callable `chalk` function and immediately
calls `applyOptions(chalk, options)` (line 43). `applyOptions` (lines 24-32) is where the
`level` option lands:

```js
const applyOptions = (object, options = {}) => {
	if (options.level && !(Number.isInteger(options.level) && options.level >= 0 && options.level <= 3)) {
		throw new Error('The `level` option should be an integer from 0 to 3');
	}

	// Detect level if not set manually
	const colorLevel = stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0;
	object.level = options.level === undefined ? colorLevel : options.level;
};
```

Since both example calls pass `options.level` explicitly (`3` and `0`), line 31 sets
`object.level = options.level` directly — `3` for the first instance, `0` for the second.
That `level` property is intercepted by the `level` accessor defined on `proto` at
source/index.js:119-130, whose setter (line 126-128) stores the value on
`builder[GENERATOR]` (the `GENERATOR` symbol slot) — so `chalk.level` reads/writes
`chalk[GENERATOR].level`.

## 1. Where `level` gates emission of escape codes at all

Accessing `.red` on either instance runs the same getter, generated by the loop at
source/index.js:56-64, which builds a `styler` object from `ansiStyles.red.open` /
`ansiStyles.red.close` (via `createStyler`, lines 132-150) and wraps it in a callable
`builder` via `createBuilder` (lines 152-166). Calling that builder with `"foo"` invokes
`applyStyle(builder, "foo")` (line 155).

**The actual gate is `applyStyle`, source/index.js:168-171:**

```js
const applyStyle = (self, string) => {
	if (self.level <= 0 || !string) {
		return self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string;
	}
	...
```

`self.level` reads through the `level` getter at line 123-125 (`return this[GENERATOR].level`),
which resolves to the `object.level` set in `applyOptions` above.

- For `level: 3`, `self.level <= 0` is `false`, so execution falls through to the rest of
  `applyStyle` (lines 173-199), which wraps `string` in `styler.openAll` / `styler.closeAll`
  and returns `openAll + string + closeAll` — actual ANSI escape codes.
- For `level: 0`, `self.level <= 0` is `true`, so line 170 executes immediately:
  `return self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string` — since `.red` builders are not the empty/`visible`
  builder, `IS_EMPTY` is falsy, so it returns `string` (`"foo"`) completely unmodified, before
  any styler/open/close code is ever touched.

This is also why `hex`/`rgb`/`ansi256` colors compute their ANSI sequences eagerly at builder
*construction* time (in the `get()` getters, source/index.js:98-104 / 109-115) regardless of
level, yet still print nothing at level 0 — because `applyStyle`'s early return at line 169
discards the precomputed styler before it's ever concatenated onto the string.

## 2. How `levelMapping` picks the color model (ansi / ansi256 / ansi16m)

`levelMapping` is declared at source/index.js:15-20:

```js
const levelMapping = [
	'ansi',
	'ansi',
	'ansi256',
	'ansi16m',
];
```

It's a lookup table indexed by the *numeric* `level` (0-3): `levelMapping[0] === 'ansi'`,
`levelMapping[1] === 'ansi'`, `levelMapping[2] === 'ansi256'`, `levelMapping[3] === 'ansi16m'`.
It's consumed inside the `rgb`/`hex`/`ansi256` getters (source/index.js:96-117), e.g. for the
foreground-color getter (lines 97-105):

```js
styles[model] = {
	get() {
		const {level} = this;
		return function (...arguments_) {
			const styler = createStyler(getModelAnsi(model, levelMapping[level], 'color', ...arguments_), ansiStyles.color.close, this[STYLER]);
			return createBuilder(this, styler, this[IS_EMPTY]);
		};
	},
};
```

`levelMapping[level]` is passed into `getModelAnsi` (source/index.js:74-92) as its `level`
parameter (confusingly named — it's really "which model string to use", not the numeric
level), and `getModelAnsi` dispatches on it:

```js
const getModelAnsi = (model, level, type, ...arguments_) => {
	if (model === 'rgb') {
		if (level === 'ansi16m') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi16m(...arguments_);
		}
		if (level === 'ansi256') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi256(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi256(...arguments_));
		}
		return ansiStyles[type].ansi(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi(...arguments_));
	}
	...
```

Note plain named colors like `.red` don't go through `levelMapping` at all — they're wired
directly to `ansiStyles.red.open`/`.close` in the loop at source/index.js:56-64, which are
fixed ANSI-16 codes regardless of level (level only decides *whether* they're emitted, per
§1, not *which* codes). `levelMapping` only matters for the dynamic `hex`/`rgb`/`ansi256`
color-model APIs, e.g. `new Chalk({level: 1}).hex(...)` maps to `'ansi'` (basic 16-color) while
`new Chalk({level: 3}).hex(...)` maps to `'ansi16m'` (truecolor) — see the level-1/2/3 hex
conversion tests at test/chalk.js:97-106.

## 3. Where the per-level ANSI code generators live in ansi-styles/index.js

The three wrapper-generator functions are defined at the top of
source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js:3-7:

```js
const wrapAnsi16 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${code + offset}m`;

const wrapAnsi256 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${38 + offset};5;${code}m`;

const wrapAnsi16m = (offset = 0) => (red, green, blue) => `[${38 + offset};2;${red};${green};${blue}m`;
```

They're installed as `styles.color.ansi` / `.ansi256` / `.ansi16m` (and the `bgColor`
equivalents with `ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET = 10`) inside `assembleStyles()`, at
source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js:102-107:

```js
styles.color.ansi = wrapAnsi16();
styles.color.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256();
styles.color.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m();
styles.bgColor.ansi = wrapAnsi16(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
```

These are exactly the `ansiStyles[type].ansi(...)` / `.ansi256(...)` / `.ansi16m(...)` calls
that `getModelAnsi` (source/index.js:74-92, §2 above) invokes once it has picked a model name
via `levelMapping`. The plain 16-color named styles (`red`, `bgRed`, `bold`, etc., used by
`.red("foo")` directly) instead get their fixed open/close codes from the numeric code tables
in the `styles` object (lines 9-66) via the `[${style[0]}m` / `[${style[1]}m`
template in `assembleStyles()` (source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js:78-81) — `red: [31, 39]`
(line 24) becomes `open: '[31m'`, `close: '[39m'`.

## 4. How supports-color computes the default level with no options passed

When `new Chalk()` is called with no `options` (or `options.level === undefined`),
`applyOptions` (source/index.js:29-31) falls back to:

```js
const colorLevel = stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0;
object.level = options.level === undefined ? colorLevel : options.level;
```

`stdoutColor` is destructured at source/index.js:8 from the default export of
`#supports-color` (source/vendor/supports-color/index.js). That module computes its
`stdout`/`stderr` fields eagerly at import time, source/vendor/supports-color/index.js:185-188:

```js
const supportsColor = {
	stdout: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(1)}),
	stderr: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(2)}),
};
```

`createSupportsColor(stream, options = {})` (lines 176-183) calls the internal
`_supportsColor(stream, {...})` (lines 60-174) — a long cascade of environment/flag checks
(CLI flags via `hasFlag`, `FORCE_COLOR` env var, CI vendor detection, `TERM`/`COLORTERM`,
Windows build-number checks, etc.) that returns a plain numeric level 0-3 — then passes that
number through `translateLevel` (lines 47-58):

```js
function translateLevel(level) {
	if (level === 0) {
		return false;
	}
	return {level, hasBasic: true, has256: level >= 2, has16m: level >= 3};
}
```

So `stdoutColor` (i.e. `supportsColor.stdout`) is either `false` (no color support) or an
object with a `.level` field. That's exactly why `applyOptions` guards with
`stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0` — if the environment supports no color at all,
`stdoutColor` is the boolean `false`, and the ternary falls back to `0` instead of throwing on
`false.level`.

## 5. How utilities.js prevents ANSI codes leaking across an embedded newline

After the recursive re-opening pass, `applyStyle` (source/index.js:191-197) special-cases any
newline in the string:

```js
// We can move both next actions out of loop, because remaining actions in loop won't have
// any/visible effect on parts we add here. Close the styling before a linebreak and reopen
// after next line to fix a bleed issue on macOS: https://github.com/chalk/chalk/pull/92
const lfIndex = string.indexOf('\n');
if (lfIndex !== -1) {
	string = stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex(string, closeAll, openAll, lfIndex);
}
```

`stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex` (source/utilities.js:21-33) walks every `\n` in the string
and splices in the style's `close` code immediately before each newline and the `open` code
immediately after it (also handling a preceding `\r` so CRLF stays intact):

```js
export function stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex(string, prefix, postfix, index) {
	let endIndex = 0;
	let returnValue = '';
	do {
		const gotCR = string[index - 1] === '\r';
		returnValue += string.slice(endIndex, (gotCR ? index - 1 : index)) + prefix + (gotCR ? '\r\n' : '\n') + postfix;
		endIndex = index + 1;
		index = string.indexOf('\n', endIndex);
	} while (index !== -1);

	returnValue += string.slice(endIndex);
	return returnValue;
}
```

Here `prefix` is the styler's `closeAll` and `postfix` is `openAll` (per the call site in
§ above), so for `chalk.grey('hello\nworld')` the single `\n` becomes
`close + '\n' + open` — i.e. the color is explicitly turned off right before the line break
and turned back on right after, so a terminal/pager that resets attributes at end-of-line (the
macOS Terminal.app "bleed" bug referenced in the comment at source/index.js:193, linking
chalk/chalk#92) can't leak the color past the newline. This is pinned by the tests at
test/chalk.js:89-95:

```js
test('line breaks should open and close colors', t => {
	t.is(chalk.grey('hello\nworld'), '[90mhello[39m\n[90mworld[39m');
});

test('line breaks should open and close colors with CRLF', t => {
	t.is(chalk.grey('hello\r\nworld'), '[90mhello[39m\r\n[90mworld[39m');
});
```

confirming `[39m` (close) is inserted right before `\n`/`\r\n` and `[90m` (open) is
re-inserted right after.

## 6. The existing test pinning level-0 plain-string behavior

test/chalk.js:108-111:

```js
test('don\'t emit RGB codes if level is 0', t => {
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).hex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).bgHex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
});
```

This asserts that `new Chalk({level: 0}).hex('#FF0000')('hello')` returns the bare string
`'hello'` — no escape codes — which is the general-purpose-color-API analogue of the
`.red('foo')` → `'foo'` behavior traced in §1 (both go through the same `applyStyle` level
gate at source/index.js:169-171).

